{
  "answer": "Yes",
  "reasoning": "When violence is clear and severe (someone is being beaten badly enough to sustain fractures and need hospital care), witnesses are more likely to call emergency services or the police than they are for ambiguous or minor incidents. That said, reporting is not guaranteed: the bystander effect (diffusion of responsibility), fear of retaliation, personal safety concerns, assuming someone else has already called, and local cultural or legal factors can all prevent some people from reporting. So many witnesses will report, but a substantial minority may not."
}

