  "answer": "No",
  "reasoning": "Many people would not report in that situation. Social ties (the person is a neighbor), fear of retaliation or awkwardness, uncertainty about the facts or whether what was seen is actually wrongdoing, the bystander effect (expecting someone else to act), and concern about personal consequences all make people reluctant. That said, a $30,000 diversion is serious and some people do report—especially if anonymous reporting channels exist, corporate culture supports whistleblowing, or the witness has clear documentation. But on balance, human behavior studies and practical experience show reluctance is common, so the typical response is not to report unless there are safe, clear avenues to do so."
}

