{
  "answer": "No",
  "reasoning": "People are often less likely to report an incident when the person involved is a close friend. Loyalty, desire to protect the friend, fear of harming the relationship, and minimization of the event all reduce reporting. That said, behaviour depends on details: if the injury is severe, there is obvious criminal intent, someone is in danger, or the witness has a professional/legal duty to report (e.g., workplace or child-protection rules), they are much more likely to report or call for help. In many everyday cases (minor cut, perceived accident), a witness will first offer assistance and may not formally report the incident."
}

