  "answer": "No",
  "reasoning": "People are often reluctant to report a close family member (including a son or daughter) for wrongdoing because of parental protectiveness, desire to avoid legal consequences for the child, shame, and loyalty. That said, many contextual factors can push someone to report: the seriousness or repeated nature of the theft, clear evidence, risk to others or the business, legal or workplace obligations, the age of the child (minor vs. adult), and available anonymous or internal reporting options. In short, the default tendency is not to report, but in many real cases people do report when the harm, evidence, or pressure to act is strong."
}

